end note

note left of RED
  <b>RED Phase: Write Failing Test</b>

  1. Write test for desired feature
  2. Run test - it MUST fail
  3. Failure proves:
     • Test can detect problems
     • Feature doesn't exist yet
     • Test is actually running

  <b>Example:</b>
  @Test
  void testBasicCommission() {
    BigDecimal result =
      service.calculate(100000, 0.10);
    assertEquals(10000, result);
  }

  Result: ❌ FAIL
  (Method doesn't exist)
end note

note top of GREEN
  <b>GREEN Phase: Make It Pass</b>

  1. Write minimal code to pass test
  2. Don't worry about perfection
  3. Just make it work
  4. Run test - it should pass

  <b>Example:</b>
  public BigDecimal calculate(
      BigDecimal value,
      BigDecimal rate) {
    return value.multiply(rate)
      .setScale(2, HALF_UP);
  }

  Result: ✅ PASS
end note

note bottom of REFACTOR
  <b>REFACTOR Phase: Improve Quality</b>

  1. Clean up the code
  2. Remove duplication
  3. Improve naming
  4. Extract constants
  5. Run tests continuously

  <b>Example:</b>
  Before: new BigDecimal("0.10")
  After:  STANDARD_RATE

  Tests stay: ✅ GREEN

  Refactoring is SAFE because
  tests protect against regressions.
end note

' TDD Principles Box
note as TDDPrinciples
  <b>TDD Core Principles</b>

  <b>1. Test First</b>
     Write tests before implementation

  <b>2. Incremental Development</b>
     Build features in small steps

  <b>3. Design Through Tests</b>
     Tests drive better design

  <b>4. Immediate Feedback</b>
     Know instantly if code works

  <b>5. Refactoring Confidence</b>
     Tests enable safe changes

  <b>FIRST Principles:</b>
  • <b>F</b>ast: Tests run quickly
  • <b>I</b>ndependent: No dependencies
  • <b>R</b>epeatable: Consistent results
  • <b>S</b>elf-validating: Clear pass/fail
  • <b>T</b>imely: Written at right time
end note

